Yes, conceptually, this captures every structural way a programming language can fail. While there are thousands of individual software bugs (CVEs) discovered every year, they all boil down to these same core structural categories.
At a fundamental computer-science level, every programming language vulnerability boils down to just two ultimate architectural mistakes:

   1. Context Confusion: The language engine fails to separate raw data from executable code.
   2. Memory/State Mismanagement: The language engine allows data to leak, overlap, or be accessed at the wrong time in computer memory.

## How New Variations Are Discovered
Even though the core categories don't change, researchers are constantly finding clever new variations within them.
For example, Prototype Pollution (Category 3) did not exist as a mainstream threat years ago. However, when JavaScript/Node.js became dominant, researchers discovered that its specific architectural design—using "prototypes" to inherit objects—could be weaponized. The category was old (Parsing and Alteration), but the specific execution method was entirely new.
## How to Use This Knowledge as a Advantage
By understanding these primary structural categories, you can spot vulnerabilities in brand-new languages long before official scanners (like Nuclei) have templates for them:

* If a new language lets you build objects from raw network text, immediately test it for Deserialization Exploits.
* If a new language interfaces directly with low-level C libraries, immediately look for Null Byte or Memory Allocation bugs.

